A||||7||Fat||Mountain Biking is Fun||||7||
Z||000000||Fat||02-12-2001||09:11 PM||||Hey dare, are there any mountain bikers around here.  I just got into the sport last year.  I bought myself a used Cannondale super V 2000.  I like it!  I've upgrade to nine speed XT components, including brakes and shifters.  I rebuilt the front shock with new bearings (tough work).  Just need to get it tuned up then I'm going to hit the trails.  I always go local.  Alum Creek trail is pretty good, but it gets muddy, definatly better in late summer and fall.  Is there anyone around Ohio who knows of any good trails (the lesser known the better)?  I'm not a downhiller, because I still have one or two working brain cells left, but I do like a good hilly trail.  Lots of ups and downs like a roller coaster.||||reg||7
Z||000001||Standard||02-12-2001||11:15 PM||||Hell yeah!  I gotta get in shape for the racing season here in Minnesota.  I race an Independent Fabrications Deluxe frame outfitted with Zokes Z2 BAM, XTR brake/shift levers, XTR rear hub, XTR rear. der, Syncros crankset and seatpost, XT bottom bracket and cassette, White Bro's Ti handlebar, Titec stem, Avid Arch Supreme brakes... benefits of working at a shop for almost 4 years  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  Also have a Kona U'Hu dual suspension, pretty much stock 'cept for a Zokes Bomber Z1 up front that I use for downtown ridin' and getting crazy.   I'll ride anything but I've really taken a liking to the ski areas around here.. I like technical stuff, too, but ya' can't beat a nice full-speed blast every once in a while  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  Of course, I'm not too sure that I have <B>any</B> working brain cells left  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Everybody wants a normal life and a cool car.. <B>Most people settle for the car</B><P>I'm not dumb.  I just have a command of thoroughly useless information<BR>        <BR>AOL: LastMinuteBastrd<BR>AIM: SavedByJebus    -- you'll understand if you watch The Simpsons :D||||reg||1
Z||000002||Fat||02-12-2001||11:28 PM||||$$$$  I'm into this stuff too much.  Always buying something new.  I like the components I have on the bike right now because 2-3 times more dough for xtr stuff doesn't make sense when you ride a heavy bike.  Someday I'd like to be fast enough to race cross country, but until then I'll be satisfied to bounce along on my little suspension bike.  I'll probably buy the who she-bang from a local shop when I upgrade.  It's good to have knowlegable people work on that type of equiptment.  Believe it or not gang, Fieros are easier to work on then high end bikes.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/cool.gif"> <P>I bet you guys have got some nice trails up there in Minnisota.  I've always wanted to take a trip out west though.  Colorado is probably too overrun.  You got any suggestions, Standard?  Where's the place to be seen riding your bike?||||reg||1
Z||000003||N_Man||02-13-2001||01:26 AM||||Mountain biking hey... gotta reply to this one.  im a mechanic at one of the bigger shops in town for a little over 4 years now.  <BR>I ride a gary fisher tequila frame (cause 'steel is real')  XTR rear der. XT front, syncros TI seat post.  RaceFace cranks, headset and ti bottom bracket.  Avid 20 brakes (cheap, work good, and SOO light).  XT is pretty much anthing else on the bike.  Oh yea and the best looking profile carbon wrapped handlebar.<P>Raceing... not my cup-o-tea, everyone at the shop who races is such a nerd about it... on the freaking wind trainer like an hour each day.  If the roads are clear enough to ride their on their road bikes.  We have one guy that refuses to get a car, he rides his bike everywhere even in the -40 temps.   I prefer the fun side of things, ya know...<P>Calgary and surrounding area has some awsome trails to ride, if the chance comes up canada IS the place to ride.||||reg||14
Z||000004||Standard||02-13-2001||02:07 AM||||Can't beat that nice grey sheen of XTR  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  Never said I was fast, I just love racing.  Best thing you can ever do.  It will improve your skills more than any amount of riding.  As for working on bikes, it's pretty easy after wrenchin' on them for 4 years  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/cool.gif"> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>We have good trails, but lots of them are too flat.  That's why I want to hit the ski area's more.  Never been out west, but <B>the</B> mountain biking mecca is Moab, Utah.  I've also heard good things about Bend, Oregon.  Check out Mountain Bike, they usually have good feature articles on places to go.<P>Here's a pic of me kinda puttin' along at a race last year.  Far left, #809<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/red_wing_small.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||frontal lobe||02-13-2001||10:31 PM||||Trick photography or are you in front?!?<P>||||reg||1
Z||000006||Standard||02-15-2001||01:38 AM||||Awww now that's mean!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> lol.<P>That was right after the start, I'm at the front of the second wave of people.  I finished mid-pack in my age group, too many hills and it had been raining almost until the start of the race.  Nice 'n muddy.||||reg||1
Z||000007||JohnnyK||02-15-2001||01:31 PM||||I also used to race.. Always wanted to race downhill. I used to ride (still have, just don't ride) titanium frame with xt/xtr components and Quadra 21R's (Hey, this was awhile ago).. Cost me too much cause I bought each thing seperately and threwer together.  I also have these IBC hydraulic brakes that I sort of wouldn't mind getting rid of (well, I would mind, they have sentimental value, but I'm not doing anything with em  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">.. Actually, I only have the rear.. Long story what happened to the front. It's been on a bike for about a day.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
